Practical 3: Installing FLOSS & Extracting Obfuscated Strings
Aim

To install FLOSS and use it to extract decoded and obfuscated strings from a binary file.

Objectives
To understand string obfuscation in binaries
To install FLOSS in a Linux environment
To extract hidden and decoded strings using FLOSS
To compare output of strings and FLOSS
Procedure (Step-by-Step on Linux)
Step 1: Create a test executable (safe sample)
Create a C file:
nano obf_sample.c
Add the following code:
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    char s1[] = "cmd.exe";
    char s2[] = "http://malicious-site.com";
    printf("Simulated execution\n");
    return 0;
}
Compile into Windows executable:
sudo apt install mingw-w64
x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc obf_sample.c -o sample.exe
Step 2: Install FLOSS (using pipx method)
Install pipx:
sudo apt install pipx
Enable pipx:
pipx ensurepath
Restart terminal or run:
source ~/.bashrc
Install FLOSS:
pipx install flare-floss
Verify installation:
floss --help
Step 3: Extract normal strings (baseline analysis)
strings sample.exe | head
Step 4: Extract obfuscated/decoded strings using FLOSS
floss sample.exe
Step 5: Save output (optional)
floss sample.exe > floss_output.txt
🎯 What to Demonstrate
strings sample.exe | head
floss sample.exe | head
🧪 Outcome

FLOSS was successfully installed using pipx and used to extract decoded and hidden strings from the executable. The tool demonstrated its ability to reveal additional information not visible through basic string extraction.

📊 Result

The analysis showed that while the strings command extracts visible text, FLOSS provides deeper insights by decoding hidden or obfuscated strings, making it a powerful tool in malware analysis.
